THE PROCESS CHANGED AT REVISION 22, AND THE CHANGE GOVERNS EVERY REVISION FROM HERE. The requirement
owner, 2026-08-24: "Let's make them atomic changes. We originally deferred for efficiency as we kept
re-reviewing the requirements mid-ADR edit. Let's just make them atomic commits. At this point I am
not interested in a stand-alone requirements review, only ensuring it is in line with the work the
ADRs reveal."

Two rulings. FIRST, every decided requirements change now lands in the SAME COMMIT as the ADR work
that reveals it - no deferral, no accumulation, and no future-tense "an amendment is owed" left
standing in an ADR while the criterion it names says something else. SECOND, THERE IS NO END-OF-PHASE
REQUIREMENTS TRUE-UP AND NO STAND-ALONE REQUIREMENTS REVIEW. This document is not reviewed as a
document in its own right before approval; the only test it has to pass is "is it in line with what
the ADRs reveal", which each atomic commit establishes for its own change at the moment it lands.
Approval is the design phase's, taken once at the end, over the requirements and the ADRs together.

WHAT THAT MEANS FOR REVISIONS 20 AND 21, WHICH THIS FILE RECORDS AS AWAITING THAT TRUE-UP: NOTHING
FURTHER IS OWED ON EITHER. Their content shipped (revision 21's at fa937a739) and the review they
were carried to no longer exists. The deferral was not a mistake - it bought efficiency while each
change was triggering a fresh mid-edit requirements review, and abolishing that review removes the
cost that made deferral worth paying. The paragraphs below are kept as the record of what each
revision changed, and their "carried to the end-of-phase true-up" sentences are spent.


