sentence"). SECOND, this is a population widening and not a new rule, so the normative-bullet count
stays at 124 - the same accounting revision 18 used when it moved this rule's population.

AC-54 builds its host WITHOUT ValidateScopes on purpose: with validation on, the ambient is declined
for a different reason and the criterion would not discriminate. It asserts two Sends in two requests
resolving DIFFERENT Scoped dependencies, which is precisely what borrowing the root would have
destroyed, plus FR-23's single latched Warning across both calls.

Revision 23 was made during the design phase and lands round-7 owner calls 1 and 8 together with
their ADR halves in one commit (batch S-a) - the second requirements change made under the atomic
process above. Call 1 is a correction to a mechanism the document stated four times; call 8 adds one
rule and one criterion.

CALL 1 - "the last descriptor" is not what MS DI resolves. FR-22.4, FR-24.3, FR-17 and C-12 all
described MS DI's resolution as taking the LAST descriptor for a service type. Probed against the
pinned Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection 10.0.10, that is false whenever a KEYED descriptor
exists: GetService<T>() resolves the last UNKEYED descriptor and ignores keyed registrations, and a
keyed descriptor reads null for ImplementationType while carrying the type on KeyedImplementationType.
Under the old wording FR-22.4 raised a startup-failing Error against a host with a keyed
IBrighterOptions registration - a multi-tenant shape ADR 0076 supports by name, and whose write-through
runs correctly. The owner chose (b) WIDEN THE RULES TO KEYED rather than (a) declare keyed
registrations out of subject, which is what both reviewers recommended. Every rule now names the
population it reads. FR-22.4 and FR-24.3 read the UNKEYED population, and the mechanism forces that
rather than taste choosing it: Brighter's factories resolve IBrighterOptions unkeyed
(ServiceProviderMapperFactory.cs:44) and ADR 0072 fixes IAmAScopeProvider's registration model as a
plain unkeyed AddSingleton, so a keyed descriptor for either is never effective. FR-22.3's candidate
scan reads keyed and unkeyed artefacts alike, and its per-parameter read gains the
[FromKeyedServices] clause. That clause is load-bearing rather than a refinement, and the probe is
the reason it is stated: a Singleton artefact taking [FromKeyedServices("k")] on a service type that
ALSO carries an unkeyed Scoped registration builds cleanly, so a rule reading the unkeyed descriptor
would report a captive dependency the container never creates.

CALL 8 - the seventh diagnostic, and why the message was not weakened instead. FR-22.4's message row
requires "the affinity the override carries", and ADR 0074 declares the delegate-registered path
reachable while stating that such an override cannot be compared and therefore cannot be reported.
The reviewer's fix was to make the value optional in FR-22.4's message. The owner chose (c) REPORT
THE REGISTRATION SHAPE ITSELF. FR-17 therefore gains a SECOND rule: where the collection holds an
affinity-carrying descriptor supplying no ImplementationInstance, validation reports a Warning naming
the registration shape - which is readable, unlike the value - and the remedy of registering a
constructed instance, an obligation ADR 0076 already places on every registrar. It is a Warning and
not an Error because nothing about such a host is broken: the override still takes effect, and what
is lost is the ability of FR-17's repeat rule to see it. FR-22.4's message row is unchanged.

WHERE THE NEW RULE LIVES, AND WHY IT IS NOT A FIFTH FR-22 RULE. This was a design detail for the
landing session rather than an owner call. It sits under FR-17 because its subject is the override
descriptor family FR-17 already reads, the residue it closes is FR-17's own, and the remedy it names
is an obligation on the registrar - which is FR-17's territory, FR-17 being the requirement that
demands every call's descriptor stay visible to validation. FR-22 is about unworkable lifetime and
opt-in CONFIGURATIONS; this rule is about a registration SHAPE. Siting it here also leaves FR-22's
"Four rules" lead correct and untouched.

SEVEN RULES, SIX CONFIGURATIONS. The count of validation messages moves from six to seven and the
severity split from three errors and three warnings to three errors and FOUR warnings - in FR-25.10,
AC-25, AC-43 and AC-44. The count of silent CONFIGURATIONS stays at six, and that is not an
oversight: the first six rules each describe a host whose behaviour departs from its configuration,
while the seventh describes a host that behaves exactly as configured and has merely made one of the
other rules blind. ADR 0074 states the distinction rather than absorbing it.

AN ACCEPTED SILENCE, DECIDED BY THE OWNER RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Widening to keyed descriptors makes a
keyed IBrighterOptions registration detectable for the first time, so whether to report it was raised
as a call of its own. The answer was NO NEW RULE: such a host works, ADR 0076 names the keyed
registration as a supported multi-tenant shape, and a warning would fire against hosts that are
behaving as intended. ADR 0074's Failure modes table carries it as an enumerated silence.

